## 4. Production processes — detailed review
This section reviews the main LA production routes and reports typical conditions, yields, by‑products, pros and cons.

### A. Homogeneous mineral‑acid hydrolysis (H2SO4 / HCl)
Mechanism
- Protonation of glycosidic bonds → hydrolysis of cellulose to glucose → dehydration to 5‑HMF → rehydration to LA + formic acid.

Typical conditions
- 160–210 °C (hydrothermal), dilute to concentrated mineral acids depending on route.

Yields
- Pure hexoses: 60–90% theoretical in optimized lab conditions.  
- Raw biomass: 30–60% practical yields due to humins and incomplete hydrolysis.

By‑products & disposition
- Formic acid (co‑product), humins (insoluble carbonaceous polymers), soluble oligomers.

Pros
- High intrinsic activity; simple catalyst; known chemistry.

Cons
- Highly corrosive streams → expensive materials & maintenance; acid recovery/neutralization costs; large acidic wastewater.

Suitability
- Historically common in lab development; high OPEX/environmental cost challenges for industrial scale.

### B. Heterogeneous solid‑acid catalysis (sulfonated carbons, zeolites, HPA supports)
Mechanism
- Surface acid sites catalyze hydrolysis and dehydration; reactions depend on catalyst dispersion and pore diffusion.

Typical conditions
- 150–220 °C; packed bed or slurry reactors.

Yields
- Model sugars: 50–90% under optimized lab conditions.  
- Real biomass: commonly 40–70% with effective pretreatment.

By‑products & disposition
- Formic acid in aqueous phase, humin deposition on catalyst (deactivation).

Pros
- Lower corrosion; catalyst reuse feasible; easier downstream separation versus mineral acid.

Cons
- Catalyst fouling (humins); regeneration required; some catalysts costlier.

Suitability
- Promising for pilots/commercial plants if regeneration & robustness handled.

### C. Biphasic systems (aqueous + organic solvent such as GVL, MIBK)
Mechanism
- Organic solvent extracts LA (and intermediates) as formed, reducing residence of reactive intermediates in water and suppressing humin formation.

Typical conditions
- 150–220 °C; organic:aqueous often 1:1 to 2:1; catalyst can be homogeneous or heterogeneous.

Yields
- Improved vs single aqueous phase: 50–80% on model substrates; 45–70% on pretreated biomass in literature examples.

By‑products & disposition
- LA partitions to organic phase; formic acid remains in aqueous phase; humins reduced.

Pros
- Higher selectivity/yield; easier product extraction; solvent recyclable.

Cons
- Solvent inventory & energy for recovery; solvent choice affects economics and safety.

Suitability
- Excellent compromise between yield, separability and environmental profile when combined with solid acids.

### D. Continuous flow (packed‑bed) & catalytic membrane reactors
Concepts
- Packed bed: continuous tubular reactors with catalyst packing; suitable for continuous solids handling with pretreated slurry.  
- Catalytic membrane reactor: combines reaction with selective product removal to reduce humin formation and shift equilibrium.

Typical conditions
- 160–200 °C; pressurized for liquid phase; WHSV/residence time tuned.

Yields
- Lab/pilot: up to 80–90% on model feeds with optimized membranes; 40–75% realistic for biomass.

Pros
- Better heat/mass transfer, scalable, catalyst reuse; steady product quality.

Cons
- Fouling & solids handling; higher CAPEX for pressure equipment & membranes.

Suitability
- Best industrial pathway when combined with biphasic solvent and solid catalysts.

### E. Emerging methods
- Ionic liquids & deep eutectic solvents (dissolve cellulose; IL recovery is an economic barrier).  
- Microwave/ultrasound acceleration (lab scale).  
- Subcritical water (solvent-driven acid catalysis; yields variable at scale).  
These remain experimental for large pilots.

