## 5. Comparison table (concise) and selection rationale
| Process | Catalyst | T (°C) | Yield (on sugar) | Yield (biomass) | Env. impact | Catalyst reuse | Scalability |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|
| Homogeneous mineral‑acid | H2SO4/HCl | 160–210 | 60–90% | 30–60% | High (acidic effluent) | No | Technically ready but high OPEX |
| Solid‑acid hetero. | Sulfonated carbon/zeolite/HPA | 150–220 | 50–90% | 40–70% | Moderate | Yes (regeneration) | Good |
| Biphasic (aqueous+organic) | Homog. or hetero. | 150–220 | 60–80% | 45–70% | Lower (reduced humins) | If hetero: yes | Promising |
| Continuous packed‑bed + biphasic | Solid acid (+membrane option) | 160–200 | 70–90% (model) | 40–75% | Lower | Yes | Best for scale‑up |

Selection rationale (summary)
- Continuous biphasic solid‑acid process chosen because it maximizes effective yield and selectivity (biphasic extraction reduces humins and protects intermediates), reduces corrosive waste (solid acid), enables catalyst reuse and regeneration, and is scalable (packed‑bed / catalytic membrane options). For India, this approach also minimizes wastewater neutralization costs and suits local feedstock logistics.

